\f1\fs22 thus predicting that beloved woollen socks would vanished instantly, uncomfortable synthetic sport socks multiplied with the persistence of unwanted dinner invitations, and socks given as presents at Christmas  would basically never even wear out, possibly because they were tossed in the drawer in their original packaging, as expressed in the First Law of Hosiery Conservation: \'93Stop gifting me underwear.\'94 It also became a tenet of economics in many footed civilisations, after they had suffered what would come to be known as Great Regifting Depressions, when vast cohorts of the population waged family wars with regifted socks, thus crashing Christmas sales forecasts for decades.\
So much for economics. Back in the world of real sciences, researchers discovered that sock disappearances created minute ripples in the natural fabric of space-time\'97so minute that they were detectable only by exceedingly sensitive instruments that could measure the disappointment of someone opening a sock drawer. These ripples, termed \'93Disappointment Waves\'94, propagated non-locally, suggesting that sock loss was a fundamental feature of reality itself.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The scientific community became truly conference-level alarmed when Disappointment Waves were shown to accumulate and lurk where dark matter had finally been proven to not exist. Mathematical formulae were devised, which included such variables as Average Sock Colour Wavelength and Sock Hole  Half-life, which led to advances in Sock Singularity Theory in physics and the Cherish Your Socks Movement in the Wellness/Religious Cult Economy.\
Self-appointed experts were staggered by the implications of how much airtime and likes they could accumulate by warning that missing socks were gathering in parallel dimensions and reaching critical sock density, which could potentially trigger a cascade failure across the multiverse. Over time sock disappearances accelerated at the same rate that podcast-hours were devoted to the crisis. White goods manufacturers began selling washing machines, dryers and curling wands that significantly reduced the incidence of textile dislocation anomalies.\
Another theory emerged that proved, beyond all rejection of a store credit, that Sock Disappointment Wave disparity is created multiverses in the first place.\
The multiverse\'92s brightest minds spent their days desperately trying to send inter-dimensional messages, not seeking the secrets of existence or the meaning of life, but simply asking if anyone, anywhere, had seen their socks. The question, \'93Has anyone out there found our missing socks?\'94 became the first received message between universes, widely broadcast in desperate hope that, somewhere, an intelligence had discovered not just where the socks had gone, but how to get them back before the cosmic laundry problem produced multi-universal heat death, due in no small part to the increasing displeasure of cold feet.
